encyclopedia :quiet_way do
  name "The Quiet Way"
  summary "The Quiet Way is a devotional discipline whose households live at the smallest resonance wake they can achieve — felt-shod, soft-spoken, every tool damped — on the teaching that the bands carry what is done into the world's fabric and a life should be careful about what it writes there. Its communities are few, sought-after as neighbors, and famous for the one loud thing they do."
  kind :culture
  subkind :belief
  status :complete
  topics :religion, :resonance, :household, :"social-structure"
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when any: { place: [:sealed_hab, :urban] }
  integration "Way households live inside ordinary settlements rather than apart, and their trades cluster where damped hands are worth wages — archives, instrument floors, weaving chambers, sickrooms; a district with a Way street schedules its noise with them, and mostly considers the bargain good"
  formal_register "Way speech is low, complete, and unhurried — the discipline treats an interruption as a spill — and its one formal occasion, the year-turning bell, is the single deliberate loudness the practice permits"
  aesthetic "Felted, waxed, and weighted: gray-felt shoes and door runners, waxed drawer slides, tool handles wrapped and pans set on cork — a Way room is furnished so that everything in it can be used silently, and the furnishing itself is the confession of faith"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "Households recognizable by their gray-felt shoes, wrapped tool " \
                "handles, and the composed economy of people who move as if the " \
                "floor were listening — which, by their teaching, it is.",
    attire: "Soft-woven working clothes with ties instead of clasps, the felt shoes " \
            "that mark the practice at a glance, and the small bell worn silenced " \
            "at the belt, its clapper bound, as the discipline's badge.",
    manner: "Low, complete, unhurried speech; work done with damped tools at a " \
            "settled pace; and a courtesy built on making room — a Way member " \
            "yields passage, waits out others' noise, and treats haste in others " \
            "with mild professional interest rather than judgment.",
    hospitality: "A Way table is laid on cloth, served in the practice's soft-handed quiet, and open to" \
                 "anyone who arrives; guests are lent felt shoes at the door in the " \
                 "same gesture other houses use for washing water, and a guest's " \
                 "ordinary loudness is absorbed with humor as the host's practice, " \
                 "not the guest's failure."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    In Kaleidos, an act sounds. The resonance bands take up what bodies and machines do, and the world's fabric — glass, bone, structure — carries the wake; the sciences read this as physics and build #{encyclopedia_ref :cell_loom, "quiet chambers"} around it. The Quiet Way reads it as ethics. The practice's teaching, kept in a short spoken rule rather than a book, holds that every action writes into the bands and the writing persists — that the world is a recording surface, that most of what a life writes is noise, and that a person can choose to write small. The discipline that follows is total and domestic: felt shoes and door runners, waxed slides and wrapped handles, speech kept low and complete, work done at the settled pace damped tools enforce. A Way room is furnished so everything in it can be used silently, and the furnishing is the confession of faith.

    The Way's households live inside ordinary settlements, and the arrangement suits both sides with an exactness the practice's founders are said to have intended. Damped hands are worth wages wherever noise is cost — the archives, the instrument floors, the weaving chambers, the sickrooms — and a district with a Way street schedules its loud work with them and borrows their members for its most delicate. The bound bell at a Way belt is the badge: a bell carried and kept silent, the discipline stated as an object. Once a year the practice permits itself loudness. At the year's turning, each Way community unbinds its bells and rings them together, one peal, full voice, and then binds them again — the teaching being that quiet chosen over a bell in the hand differs from quiet imposed by an empty one, and that the bands should carry, once a year, proof of exactly what is being withheld the rest of it. Neighbors attend the peal in numbers. The Way, asked why the one loudness draws the biggest crowd its quiet ever gathers, answers in character: the writing is small, so the readers lean in.
  PROSE

  cue "The gray-felt shoes wait in a row inside the doorway, the guest is handed a pair with the same gesture other houses use for washing water, and the meal that follows is the quietest crowded table the visitor has sat at."
  cue "At the year's turning the Way street unbinds its bells and rings one full-voiced peal together — then binds the clappers again, and the district that gathered to hear it walks home through the resumed hush."
  affordance "A Way community is a standing supply of the most valuable hands in noise-costed work — archives, instrument floors, weaving chambers, sickrooms — and a neighborhood that keeps one gains a scheduling partner for everything delicate."
  pressure "The discipline is total, so friction arrives through ordinary life — the loud trade next door, the child drawn to the noisy world, the emergency that demands shouting — and the Way's standing answer, that the rule serves the writing and breaks for a life in danger, is tested and retold in every generation."
  variation "Urban Way streets live embedded in districts and hire out their quiet; the few sealed-hab Way communities keep whole small habs on the discipline, and instrument services book their guest rooms years out."
  variation "The bound bell varies by community — cast, salvaged, or inherited — and a Way member's funeral rings it unbound once, the life's single personal peal, before the bell passes clapper-bound to an heir."
end
